#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49be66f3-ba66-5919-b1db-61bdeb019eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/70dfc0c8412bfae3bdbb7128fc021199f11d63d6f4ee264bea20da70dd18316c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293906} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7d6100-079c-5012-9b5f-037a5687e6cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/09b2ae9333118de0621a5f29610b6a83689fadaeb9c05e7a30b2799b12a1bd58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293907} {:answer/id #uuid "708ee9ac-745d-55db-b410-f1f4aab15194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ead70b80d9256e1211addc404613f2dfe4a19baf822cbd77f429572ffc9a3a6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293908} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8adda5-0333-5dd2-8fb3-c3aec8bca265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/5a1923eec2b66ef4eb861ec42b00ea48a3285aaaf3255c42d12c001c9cde32f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293909} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbcd6fb-ec6e-579f-beca-1eaad44b5f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/23f63717c8d356c698137359824112511b0bbd5ec04c59e47d22500d842d03b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49be66f3-ba66-5919-b1db-61bdeb019eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/70dfc0c8412bfae3bdbb7128fc021199f11d63d6f4ee264bea20da70dd18316c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293906} :answer-field/id #uuid "7099fd12-65a9-5316-9ba3-b4fd5bb3e69a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04e79ef7-7090-527f-95f0-0f13ae5b3650" :question/math-academy-id "q-160627" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/assets/0bd98626bc49cc3c58e503d5a7373b6af0fcce721cfdf82ff372eaa57d157dda.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/assets/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/assets/d5ff1c1a7c99b08d04d2ac58d9f8195ee29c8b564873a9abfb2635e93cec1968.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e642302b-2c97-566a-bc78-c5cc15eeb8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302262} {:answer/id #uuid "abe73f35-7b0d-56f3-9aac-e923a6b1fe1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302263} {:answer/id #uuid "a8032f23-fcb4-53dc-a28d-542950709418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302264} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4fe7c8-beeb-5d20-a2b9-7a1dde933a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265} {:answer/id #uuid "069f3ebf-61eb-519c-8cd0-87d6077ab52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4fe7c8-beeb-5d20-a2b9-7a1dde933a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265} :answer-field/id #uuid "60f038ee-6e0a-5901-9bfb-2e937307a9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "805b41fe-1938-5f5c-95ff-fff97c430efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160648" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88da30c2-feaa-5d94-9c98-e41fd1ed9ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091104} {:answer/id #uuid "93a9126c-4c6c-5b2d-95c6-cfed9b180113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091105} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9a95e0-ae9b-5161-a560-ee947bd76206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091106} {:answer/id #uuid "02bb3447-584b-5dae-af10-da7e71bffad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218609110