s a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/assets/e941207a92a2b688428c55be3bc17856ea47fa2e11db8e3dc571b88ebbe8e3b1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9802b365-7849-5ac3-af4f-e4bd39cefdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283741} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a31c83-b3a1-5073-806a-a58a0908adf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283742} {:answer/id #uuid "01439c2d-20be-51cb-a6d5-14c759a693c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283743} {:answer/id #uuid "2d18a0db-cd28-5fed-a48b-a8c33b8bc97c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283744} {:answer/id #uuid "08e587ff-2645-5295-bf13-4780ed8f23ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3a31c83-b3a1-5073-806a-a58a0908adf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283742} :answer-field/id #uuid "02d46356-dfea-504a-85ae-dc2836b462a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e62f917-e247-5e01-9129-0fa18b8a8257" :question/math-academy-id "q-8394" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,0),(1,5),(2,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-5,1)$\n- $(0,-2)$\n- $(2,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,0),(-5,1),(-3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
